Relevant columns and how we will use them  

event_date:           filter to the single day we are interested in ( '20210102' ).  
event_name:           keep only page‑view events ( event_name = 'page_view' ).  
event_params:         UNNEST to reach the key‑value pairs and pull the page URL that lives in the parameter whose key = 'page_location'.  
                     – we will also find other parameters (page_title, page_referrer, etc.) but only page_location is needed here.  

Important conditions we will need  

event_date         = '20210102'  
event_name         = 'page_view'  
param.key          = 'page_location'  

Page‑type rules to implement on page_location (lower‑cased for case‑insensitive matching)  

• Split the URL on “/”; make sure the array length ≥ 5.  
• Grab segment 4 or 5 (SAFE_OFFSET(3) or SAFE_OFFSET(4)).  
• Check that one of those segments contains (case‑insensitive) one of the category keywords supplied.  
• PDP = last segment of the URL contains “+”.  
• PLP = last segment does NOT contain “+”.  

Ten exploration‑to‑answer queries
(they all return max 20 rows, each illustrates one additional building block)

